module bindings.rust.index_operator_without_bounds_check;

// The std::ops::Index and std::ops::IndexMut contracts: the indexing operator
// panics when the index is out of bounds, and it is the implementation that
// provides the check -- Vec and [T] compare against their length before
// touching memory. A container that implements either trait by computing the
// element address with raw-pointer arithmetic straight from the index
// parameter, with no comparison anywhere in the method body, turns every
// container[index] in safe caller code into an unchecked read or write at an
// arbitrary offset from the heap allocation.
//
// The trait's required method names -- index, index_mut -- are fixed by the
// standard library documentation, so the entrypoint is identifiable without
// knowing the crate. The methods that can compute an element address from an
// index are the pointer-arithmetic primitives on *mut T / *const T: offset,
// add, sub and their wrapping_ and byte_ variants, matched exactly so a
// same-suffix method on another type (addr_of, checked_add) does not satisfy
// the clause. Any bounds relation the implementation establishes has to
// compare the index against something, and the Rust frontend emits no
// structured token for a comparison expression, so a < or > character in the
// method body's text is the only carrier of "a comparison exists here".
//
// Residual, in the false-negative direction only: a comparison inside a
// comment or a turbofish in the body satisfies the negation, and a check
// established outside the method -- a checked wrapper routing to an unchecked
// inner helper -- is invisible to a function-scoped binding. That is the
// standard delegation limit, not specific to this shape.
binding indexOperatorWithoutBoundsCheck {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function" and node.context.language == "rust" and node.context.name in ["index", "index_mut"] and node.context.call in ["offset", "add", "sub", "wrapping_offset", "wrapping_add", "wrapping_sub", "byte_offset", "byte_add", "byte_sub"] and not (containsAny(node.token, ["<", ">"]))
  emit issue code.IndexOperatorWithoutBoundsCheck at node
}
